1
00:00:00,132 --> 00:00:03,437
{happy}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,437 --> 00:00:05,460
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:10,305
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:16,694
In the first nine days of November, foreign investors put a net fifty-four thousand

5
00:00:16,694 --> 00:00:19,558
crore rupees or so into Indian shares.

6
00:00:19,558 --> 00:00:23,443
If that holds, it would be their biggest month since September 2024.

7
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:26,800
Yet in September and October, they were selling.

8
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:27,941
So what changed?

9
00:00:28,783 --> 00:00:32,781
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah, and today I'll walk you through who is buying India, and why

10
00:00:32,781 --> 00:00:33,785
they came back now.

11
00:00:33,785 --> 00:00:35,676
{fluent}First, who are these buyers?

12
00:00:35,676 --> 00:00:41,150
Foreign portfolio investors, or F-P-Is, are overseas funds that buy shares and bonds

13
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:42,621
in Indian companies.

14
00:00:42,621 --> 00:00:46,004
They hold a slice of a business without running it.

15
00:00:46,004 --> 00:00:49,072
In September and October, they sold more than they bought.

16
00:00:49,072 --> 00:00:52,390
In November, that flipped.

17
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:56,276
Between the second and the ninth, six trading days, they bought a net fifty-three

18
00:00:56,276 --> 00:01:01,731
thousand, seven hundred and thirty-nine crore rupees of Indian shares, according to

19
00:01:01,731 --> 00:01:04,393
data reported by the depositories.

20
00:01:04,393 --> 00:01:07,786
The month is not over, so that figure will keep moving.

21
00:01:08,733 --> 00:01:10,681
Why did they come back?

22
00:01:10,681 --> 00:01:11,666
Start with the trigger.

23
00:01:11,666 --> 00:01:16,194
One market strategist says it was a sell-off in shares linked to artificial

24
00:01:16,194 --> 00:01:22,605
intelligence, or A-I, in the United States, South Korea, Taiwan and Japan.

25
00:01:22,605 --> 00:01:23,897
Here is how that works.

26
00:01:23,897 --> 00:01:28,445
A global fund has a fixed pile of money to spread across countries.

27
00:01:28,445 --> 00:01:33,783
When the markets full of A-I shares fall, some of that money looks elsewhere.

28
00:01:33,783 --> 00:01:39,361
India has far fewer of those shares, so some funds may see it as a way to spread

29
00:01:39,361 --> 00:01:40,551
their money differently.

30
00:01:40,551 --> 00:01:42,983
That is one strategist's reading, not a certainty.

31
00:01:43,946 --> 00:01:45,217
Second, oil.

32
00:01:45,217 --> 00:01:50,881
After the American midterm elections, hopes rose that tension with Iran would ease,

33
00:01:50,881 --> 00:01:51,985
and oil prices fell.

34
00:01:51,985 --> 00:01:56,469
India buys most of its oil from abroad, so cheaper oil helps its economy.

35
00:01:57,235 --> 00:01:57,964
Third, price.

36
00:01:57,964 --> 00:02:03,876
After weeks of selling, one strategist said Indian shares looked reasonable, if not

37
00:02:03,876 --> 00:02:04,476
cheap.

38
00:02:04,104 --> 00:02:05,540
That is his view, not ours.

39
00:02:06,331 --> 00:02:09,135
Now, how does a fund's decision reach the rupee?

40
00:02:09,135 --> 00:02:10,959
Picture a fund in London.

41
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:12,218
It holds dollars.

42
00:02:12,218 --> 00:02:16,339
To buy Indian shares, it first has to sell those dollars and buy rupees.

43
00:02:16,339 --> 00:02:20,819
Do that on a large scale, and more people are asking for rupees.

44
00:02:20,819 --> 00:02:22,363
That tends to lift the rupee.

45
00:02:22,363 --> 00:02:26,870
A stronger rupee means fewer rupees are needed to buy one dollar.

46
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:32,677
At the end of October, it took about ninety-five rupees to buy a dollar.

47
00:02:32,677 --> 00:02:37,308
By the ninth of November, it took about ninety-four.

48
00:02:37,308 --> 00:02:41,913
Many things move the rupee, so we cannot credit the inflows alone.

49
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:43,870
So, a quick recap:

50
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:49,115
shares linked to A-I fell elsewhere, oil eased, and Indian prices looked more

51
00:02:49,115 --> 00:02:51,738
reasonable, so foreign money moved back.

52
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:53,543
What do we not know?

53
00:02:53,543 --> 00:02:56,041
Six trading days is a short run.

54
00:02:56,041 --> 00:03:00,640
This kind of money can leave as quickly as it arrives, and in September and October,

55
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,563
it was leaving.

56
00:03:01,563 --> 00:03:07,469
Dealers are watching oil, and the chance of a rate rise in the United States, which

57
00:03:07,469 --> 00:03:10,305
could pull money back towards American bonds.

58
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:16,050
Totals also differ between data providers, because they count exchange trades and

59
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:18,178
new share sales differently.

60
00:03:18,998 --> 00:03:19,778
To sum up:

61
00:03:19,778 --> 00:03:25,830
foreign investors have returned to Indian shares in a hurry, mostly, by analysts'

62
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:31,882
accounts, because of what happened elsewhere, a sell-off in A-I shares and easing

63
00:03:31,882 --> 00:03:32,482
oil.

64
00:03:32,094 --> 00:03:34,639
Whether they stay depends on those same things.

65
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:41,766
Before you go, we looked at the other side of this story on the twenty-second of

66
00:03:41,766 --> 00:03:47,842
October, in our episode on why foreign investors were selling Indian shares.

67
00:03:47,842 --> 00:03:52,320
And on the sixth of November, we explained the sell-off in A-I shares.

68
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:54,475
The links are in the description.

69
00:03:54,475 --> 00:03:57,982
{happy}Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

